            <h3>Receive and Analyze the Rejection</h3>
            <p>You receive the official resolución denegatoria from the consulate. This is your starting point. Note the exact date — your 30-day deadline begins here.</p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--red); font-weight: 600;">Timeline: Day 1</p>
          </div>

          <div class="msv__step-card" data-step="2">
            <h3>Identify Grounds for Appeal</h3>
            <p>Determine whether your appeal is based on a legal error, factual misinterpretation, procedural violation, or incorrect application of requirements. This is critical—your appeal must cite specific grounds.</p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--red); font-weight: 600;">Timeline: Days 2–5</p>
          </div>

          <div class="msv__step-card" data-step="3">
            <h3>Prepare the Appeal Document</h3>
            <p>Write a formal <strong>escrito de recurso de alzada</strong>. This is a formal letter that clearly states your identity, the decision being appealed, your legal grounds, and what outcome you're requesting. Professional drafting is strongly recommended.</p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--red); font-weight: 600;">Timeline: Days 6–15</p>
          </div>

          <div class="msv__step-card" data-step="4">
            <h3>Gather Supporting Evidence</h3>
            <p>Collect documents that support your appeal. This might include additional proof of income, corrected document translations, explanatory letters, or evidence that the consulate overlooked information in your original application.</p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--red); font-weight: 600;">Timeline: Days 10–20</p>
          </div>

          <div class="msv__step-card" data-step="5">
            <h3>Submit to the Relevant Authority</h3>
            <p>File your appeal with the <strong>Subdelegación del Gobierno</strong> in the jurisdiction of the consulate that rejected you (or as specified in your rejection letter). Submit in person, by certified mail, or electronically if allowed. Keep proof of submission.</p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--red); font-weight: 600;">Timeline: Day 28–30 (must be within 30 days)</p>
          </div>

          <div class="msv__step-card" data-step="6">
            <h3>Wait for Administrative Decision</h3>
            <p>The authority reviews your appeal (typically 2–4 months). Silence after 3 months is generally treated as a denial. You should receive written notification of the outcome—either an approval, denial, or request for additional information.</p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--red); font-weight: 600;">Timeline: Months 2–4</p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── What to Include in Your Appeal ──────────────── -->
    <section class="msv__section--dark">
      <div class="msv__container--narrow">
        <div class="msv__section-hd">
          <h2>What to Include in Your Appeal Document</h2>
          <p>Your escrito de recurso de alzada must contain specific elements to be legally valid and persuasive.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Required Elements of the Appeal</h3>
        <ul style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
          <li><strong>Your identification:</strong> Full name, nationality, passport number, date of birth, and current address</li>
          <li><strong>The specific decision being appealed:</strong> Reference the date of the rejection and the consulate that issued it</li>
          <li><strong>Clear statement of grounds:</strong> Explain precisely why you believe the decision was wrong. Was it a legal error? Factual misinterpretation? Procedural violation? Be specific.</li>
          <li><strong>Detailed arguments:</strong> Present logical, well-reasoned arguments. Don't simply assert that the consulate was wrong—explain why and cite relevant laws or regulations</li>
          <li><strong>Supporting evidence:</strong> Attach documents that support your argument. These might include additional financial records, corrected translations, expert opinions, or proof that the consulate overlooked information</li>
          <li><strong>What you're requesting:</strong> Clearly state what outcome you want (e.g., "overturn the rejection and approve the visa")</li>
          <li><strong>Your signature:</strong> The appeal must be signed by you or your legal representative</li>
          <li><strong>Date:</strong> The date you're submitting the appeal</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--info">
          <span class="msv__alert-icon">💡</span>
          <p><strong>Professional drafting matters:</strong> Administrative appeals are formal legal documents. Poor writing, weak arguments, or missing elements significantly reduce your chances of success. A professional advisor or immigration lawyer should review or draft your escrito de recurso de alzada. The cost (typically €500–€1,000) is an investment in your success.</p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Grounds for a Successful Appeal ──────────────── -->
    <section class="msv__section">
      <div class="msv__container--narrow">
        <div class="msv__section-hd">
          <h2>Realistic Grounds for a Successful Appeal</h2>
          <p>Not all appeals succeed. Understanding what the administrative authorities will consider valid grounds is essential.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">Appeals Most Likely to Succeed</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Procedural errors by the consulate:</strong> The consulate didn't follow the correct process, missed a deadline, or failed to properly notify you of missing documents</li>
          <li><strong>Misinterpretation of your documents:</strong> The consulate misunderstood or incorrectly evaluated evidence you provided (e.g., incorrectly calculating your income, misreading a bank statement, or doubting a legitimate document's authenticity without justification)</li>
          <li><strong>Failure to consider all evidence:</strong> You provided critical documents that the consulate overlooked or dismissed without explanation</li>
          <li><strong>Incorrect application of income thresholds:</strong> The consulate miscalculated or misapplied the €1,800/month minimum requirement (e.g., not accounting for certain income sources correctly)</li>
          <li><strong>Discriminatory treatment:</strong> Evidence that the decision was based on your nationality, age, or other protected characteristic rather than objective criteria</li>
          <li><strong>Arbitrary or unreasonable decision:</strong> The rejection lacks a reasonable basis or contradicts established consular practice</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Appeals Unlikely to Succeed</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>You genuinely don't meet the requirements:</strong> If your income truly falls below €1,800/month or you lack required health insurance, an appeal won't fix this—reapplication with corrected documents is your path</li>
          <li><strong>The consulate's interpretation is reasonable:</strong> Even if you disagree, if the consulate's reading of the rules is defensible, an appeal is unlikely to overturn it</li>
          <li>Appeals based solely on emotional arguments or complaints about the difficulty of meeting requirements generally fail</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--warn">
          <span class="msv__alert-icon">⚠️</span>
          <p><strong>Honest assessment matters:</strong> Before investing time and money in an appeal, critically evaluate whether you have genuine legal or factual grounds. If the rejection was because you didn't meet requirements, an appeal will likely fail. Professional advisors can help you assess this realistically.</p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Realistic Expectations ──────────────────────── -->
    <section class="msv__section--cream">
      <div class="msv__container--narrow">
        <div class="msv__section-hd">
          <h2>Realistic Expectations: Success Rates and Outcomes</h2>
          <p>What are your real chances of a successful appeal?</p>
        </div>

        <p>Spanish administrative authorities do not publish official NLV appeal success rates, so exact figures are unavailable. However, based on reported outcomes:</p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">What We Know</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Appeals succeed when based on genuine procedural or interpretation errors—perhaps 40–60% of well-argued appeals</li>
          <li>Appeals fail when the rejection was justified by the applicant not meeting requirements—upwards of 85% failure rate</li>
          <li>Appeals based on factual misinterpretation (e.g., the consulate wrongly calculated your income) have the highest success rates</li>
          <li>Many applicants find reapplication with corrected documents faster and more successful than appealing a justified rejection</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--info">
          <span class="msv__alert-icon">💡</span>
          <p><strong>The practical reality:</strong> If you didn't genuinely meet the requirements when you applied, appeal odds are poor. If the consulate made a demonstrable error in evaluating your application, your odds are much better. An experienced advisor can assess your specific situation and give you honest odds before you invest in an appeal.</p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Reapplying After Rejection ──────────────────── -->
    <section class="msv__section">
      <div class="msv__container--narrow">
        <div class="msv__section-hd">
          <h2>Reapplying After Rejection: The Second Attempt</h2>
          <p>If you decide to reapply rather than appeal—or while waiting for your appeal decision—here's what you need to know.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">Key Points About Reapplication</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>No waiting period:</strong> You can reapply immediately after rejection. There's no required waiting period before submitting a new application.</li>
          <li><strong>You must fix the issues:</strong> Reapplying with the same documents will result in the same rejection. Identify what went wrong and address it directly.</li>
          <li><strong>Start fresh:</strong> You're essentially submitting a new application, not amending the rejected one. This means a new application fee, potentially new documents, and the full processing timeline (3–6 months).</li>
          <li><strong>Cost implications:</strong> You'll pay the full consulate fee again (€80–€150), possibly new translations, and potentially professional support costs again.</li>
          <li><strong>Higher success potential:</strong> Many applicants find reapplication more successful than appeal because they can fix the actual problems rather than fighting about interpretation.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">What to Fix Before Reapplying</h3>

        <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 2rem;">
          <div>
            <h4 style="color: var(--navy); font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">If rejected for income:</h4>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.92rem;">
              <li>Provide additional proof of income (rental income, investment dividends, pension statements)</li>
              <li>Re-calculate to ensure you clearly meet the €1,800/month minimum</li>
              <li>Include 12 months of bank statements (not just 3)</li>
              <li>Provide a letter from your bank confirming average monthly balance and income sources</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h4 style="color: var(--navy); font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">If rejected for health insurance:</h4>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.92rem;">
              <li>Obtain a new policy that explicitly states it meets Spanish requirements</li>
              <li>Include a letter from the insurer confirming coverage in Spain and minimum benefit levels</li>
              <li>Ensure the policy covers emergency and non-emergency care in Spain</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 2rem; margin-top: 1.5rem;">
          <div>
            <h4 style="color: var(--navy); font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">If rejected for missing documents:</h4>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.92rem;">
              <li>Request a detailed list from the consulate of which documents were inadequate</li>
              <li>Obtain corrected or re-translated versions</li>
              <li>Ensure all documents are properly apostilled and dated</li>
              <li>Include a cover letter explaining what changed or what you're providing newly</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h4 style="color: var(--navy); font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">If rejected for vague reasons:</h4>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.92rem;">
              <li>Request written clarification from the consulate</li>
              <li>Have a professional review your original application for potential weaknesses</li>
              <li>Strengthen every section of your application, even if not explicitly cited as a problem</li>
              <li>Consider professional support for the reapplication</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

