    <section class="msv__section--cream">
      <div class="msv__container--narrow">
        <div class="msv__section-hd">
          <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About NLV Appeals</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__faq-list">
          <details>
            <summary>What is a recurso de alzada for NLV visa appeals?</summary>
            <div class="msv__faq-answer">
              <p>A recurso de alzada is the formal administrative appeal process in Spain that allows you to challenge a visa refusal decision. It's your legal right and is separate from reapplication. You file an appeal (escrito de recurso de alzada) with the authority above the consulate that rejected you, typically the Subdelegación del Gobierno. The appeal is reviewed by an administrative body independent of the original decision-maker.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>How long do I have to appeal an NLV refusal?</summary>
            <div class="msv__faq-answer">
              <p>You have exactly 30 days from the date of the official notification (resolución denegatoria) to file your appeal. This deadline is absolute and non-negotiable. After 30 days, you lose the right to appeal permanently. You can still reapply, but you cannot appeal. Start the process immediately if you plan to appeal.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>Where do I submit my recurso de alzada?</summary>
            <div class="msv__faq-answer">
              <p>You typically submit your appeal to the Subdelegación del Gobierno (Government Subdelegation) in the jurisdiction of the consulate that rejected you. Your rejection letter should specify where to file. You can submit in person, by certified mail, or electronically if the authority accepts online submissions. Keep proof of submission for your records.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>What are the main grounds for a successful NLV appeal?</summary>
            <div class="msv__faq-answer">
              <p>Successful appeals are typically based on: (1) procedural errors by the consulate (e.g., not following the proper process), (2) misinterpretation of your documents (e.g., wrongly calculating your income), (3) failure to consider evidence you provided, (4) incorrect application of the income threshold or other requirements, or (5) discriminatory treatment. Appeals based on not meeting the requirements are unlikely to succeed unless there was a clear error in how those requirements were assessed.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>Should I appeal or reapply after an NLV rejection?</summary>
            <div class="msv__faq-answer">
              <p>Appeal if you believe the consulate made an error in evaluating your application or if there was a procedural problem. Reapply if you genuinely didn't meet the requirements (e.g., income below €1,800/month) or if your documents were wrong/inadequate. Some applicants do both: file an appeal while preparing a new application with corrected documents. An experienced advisor can help you assess which strategy is best for your specific situation.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>How long does it take to get a decision on an NLV appeal?</summary>
            <div class="msv__faq-answer">
              <p>Administrative authorities typically review appeals within 2–4 months. However, timeframes can vary. Silence (no decision) after 3 months is generally treated as a denial under Spanish administrative law. You should receive written notification of the outcome—approval, denial, or a request for additional information. Some authorities may take longer during busy periods.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>What happens if I don't file an appeal within the 30-day deadline?</summary>
            <div class="msv__faq-answer">
              <p>Once the 30-day deadline expires, you permanently lose the right to appeal. Your only option is reapplication. There is no appeals deadline extension, no exceptions, and no second chance to file an appeal. This is why it's critical to act quickly if you decide to challenge the rejection.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long do I have to appeal an NLV refusal?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>You typically have one month from the date of the refusal decision to file an administrative appeal (recurso de reposición or recurso de alzada). This deadline is strict — missing it forecloses the administrative appeal route. Check the refusal letter carefully for the specific deadline.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What should I do immediately after receiving an NLV refusal?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>First: read the refusal letter carefully and note the specific reasons cited. Second: note the appeal deadline. Third: contact an immigration specialist as quickly as possible. Do not delay — options narrow with time. Fourth: gather any additional documentation that addresses the refusal grounds.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is it better to appeal or reapply after an NLV refusal?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>It depends on the reason for refusal. If the refusal was based on a documentable error or missing evidence that you can provide quickly, an appeal addressing those specific points can work. If the issues are more fundamental (e.g., income genuinely fell short), reapplying with a strengthened application is usually more effective.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        </div>
      </div>
